/* The handoff draws this bar 29 times across 10 of its 23 screens and always
 * the same way: a pill track with a plain fill inside it, clipped by the
 * track's own overflow.
 *
 *     <div style="height: 6px; border-radius: 999px; background: #f0ecf7;
 *                 overflow: hidden;">
 *       <div style="width: 78%; height: 100%; background: #6ba7c0;"></div>
 *     </div>
 *
 * HEIGHT IS 6px, and that is a choice among the handoff's own rather than a
 * value it states once. Counted over the 26 of those 29 that give a height
 * inline: 6px ten times, 5px six, 7px six, and one each at 8, 9, 11 and 12.
 * 6 is the mode, it is what the Splice Chamber forecast rows use - the bars
 * this game's screens need first - and the README prescribes no bar height
 * at all, so there is nothing more authoritative to defer to.
 *
 * THE TRACK FILL IS --hairline AND IT IS NOT AN EXACT QUOTE. The handoff's
 * #f0ecf7 is in no token; measured in summed channel distance it is 10 from
 * --hairline (#ece7f6), 12 from --divider (#f4f1fa) and 23 from
 * --surface-sunk (#f8f6fc). --hairline is nearest, and 10 is inside the
 * range Tokens.uss's own --violet-edge note treats as indistinguishable
 * (it calls 14 "would flatten the edge into the fill"). Adding an
 * eighteenth near-miss violet to the token layer for a 10-step difference
 * on a 6px track would be the drift that verify-uss-tokens.sh exists to
 * stop, so this reads the token.
 *
 * THE FILL HAS NO RADIUS OF ITS OWN, exactly as the handoff's does not. The
 * track clips it, so the bar's two ends are the track's pill ends at every
 * fill, and a full bar is a solid pill rather than a pill with square
 * corners poking out of it. That is the half of this component a still frame
 * CAN check, and the Components fixture renders 0 and 1 adjacent for it.
 * Confirmed there: `overflow: hidden` in UI Toolkit clips to the border
 * radius and not merely to the box, so a fill of 1 comes back with the
 * track's rounded ends rather than square corners.
 */
.progress-bar {
    flex-direction: column;
}

.progress-bar__track {
    /* A row, so the fill takes the track's full 6px height from the default
     * cross-axis stretch rather than from a percentage that would need the
     * parent's height to already be resolved. */
    flex-direction: row;
    height: 6px;
    /* 3px, NOT --radius-pill, AND THAT IS MEASURED. The handoff writes
     * `border-radius: 999px` on every one of these tracks, and in CSS a
     * radius that large on a 6px-tall box clamps proportionally to a
     * semicircular end - a pill. UI Toolkit does not clamp it that way.
     * Captured at 999px on this exact 6px x 325px track, the ends taper as
     * an ellipse over about 58px each: 2px tall at the end, 4px by 14px in,
     * and not full height until 58px in. The bar reads as a lens rather
     * than as a bar, and at fill 0.08 the whole fill lands inside the
     * taper. 3px is half the track's height, which is the pill the handoff
     * asked for, written as the literal this renderer needs.
     *
     * --radius-pill IS NOT WRONG, it is just not a pill below about 2x its
     * own value in either axis. Theme.uss's `.pill` and TimerChip.uss both
     * read it on elements around 21px tall, where the same arithmetic
     * predicts a visible lens; neither is in this task's scope and neither
     * has been looked at. */
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: var(--hairline);
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* Width is set inline, in percent, by ProgressBar.Fill. */
.progress-bar__fill {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    background-color: var(--violet);
}

/* The other five families the handoff fills these bars with. Counted over
 * the 19 of the 29 whose fill this reading could resolve: green 4, teal 3,
 * coral 3, violet 3, amber 2, mute 1 - so every one of them is here, and
 * none of them is here because it looked like a nice set.
 *
 * VIOLET IS THE DEFAULT ABOVE AND IT IS --violet, NOT --violet-light, even
 * though the handoff's own bars fill with #8878cf (--violet-light) twice
 * against #7a6ac0 (--violet) once. Three samples decide nothing, and the
 * token table calls --violet the primary brand and --violet-light "CTA
 * gradient start, selection ring" - so the bar takes the brand colour and
 * --violet-light stays where the table put it. */
.progress-bar--teal  .progress-bar__fill { background-color: var(--teal); }
.progress-bar--green .progress-bar__fill { background-color: var(--green); }
.progress-bar--coral .progress-bar__fill { background-color: var(--coral); }
.progress-bar--amber .progress-bar__fill { background-color: var(--amber); }
.progress-bar--mute  .progress-bar__fill { background-color: var(--mute-soft); }
